          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Swapping protein powder for all the flour</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Protein powder has no starch to hold water and little structure of its own. Replacing more than about a quarter to a third of the flour usually gives a dry, rubbery bake. Keep some flour, oats or almond flour in the recipe.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Baking with pure whey isolate</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Isolate sets fast and has almost no fat to soften it, so it is the type most likely to turn springy and dry in the oven. A whey and casein blend is far more forgiving for muffins, cakes and cookies.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not adding extra moisture</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Protein absorbs more liquid than flour. Add roughly two to four tablespoons of liquid per scoop, or use Greek yogurt, banana, pumpkin or applesauce, and match the batter to the thickness of the original recipe.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Baking at the original temperature and time</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Protein browns and dries faster than flour. Lower the oven by about 25 degrees Fahrenheit and check five to ten minutes early. Pull muffins when a toothpick shows a few moist crumbs rather than none.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Keeping all the sugar with a flavored powder</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most flavored powders are already sweetened with sucralose, stevia or monk fruit, and the sweetness survives baking. Cut the recipe's sugar when you use one, or bake with an unflavored powder and keep the sugar as written.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

