            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the best protein powder for baking?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  A whey and casein blend bakes best because whey alone dries out and casein holds moisture. PEScience Select, Devotion and Quest all use blends. Pure casein such as Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard Casein is best for brownies and no-bake recipes, while unflavored whey suits pancakes and pea protein is the vegan option for strongly flavored bakes.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can you bake with whey protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes, but whey works best in quick, thin batters such as pancakes, waffles and crepes. In longer bakes it tends to turn dry and rubbery. Replace only a quarter to a third of the flour, add extra liquid or yogurt, lower the oven about 25 degrees Fahrenheit and check early. A blend with casein is more forgiving.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does baking protein powder destroy the protein?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  No. Heat denatures protein, but digestion does that anyway, and the amino acids remain. The Maillard browning reaction can make a modest share of lysine unavailable, more so with high heat and long bakes. A normally baked muffin keeps the large majority of its usable protein, so baking is a reasonable way to eat more protein.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein powder can I substitute for flour?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Start by replacing about 25 to 33 percent of the flour by weight with protein powder. Add roughly two to four tablespoons of extra liquid per scoop, or a moist ingredient such as yogurt or banana. Recipes that use only protein powder are usually developed around one specific product and do not transfer well between brands.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is casein or whey better for baking?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Casein is better for dense, moist bakes such as brownies, cheesecake and no-bake bars, because it absorbs liquid and sets softly. Whey is better for pancakes and waffles, where it cooks quickly. For muffins, cookies and mug cakes, a blend of the two gives the most reliable texture.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can you bake with plant protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. Pea and plant blends absorb more liquid than whey and bake dense, so they suit brownies, cookies, banana bread and anything with cocoa, peanut butter or spices to cover their earthy taste. Add extra liquid and expect a heavier crumb. Plant proteins tend to test higher for heavy metals, so choose a brand that documents its testing.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can you bake with collagen peptides?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  You can stir collagen into batters as a protein top-up, but it cannot replace flour or whey because it adds no structure and dissolves completely. It is also an incomplete protein, low in leucine and lacking tryptophan. Use it alongside a whey, casein or plant powder rather than as the main protein in a recipe.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

